    //             AutoScalingEnabled: true
}
```

### What are the trade-offs?

The trade-offs are reduced customization, operational vendor lock-in, and potential cost cross-over for large steady workloads.

```text
Trade-off 1: Reduced customization
  - Curated set of configuration options
  - Cannot run arbitrary Flink versions immediately
  - Cannot install custom native libraries or sidecars
  - May not support niche connectors

Trade-off 2: Operational vendor lock-in
  - Auto-scaling, checkpoint management, deployment are AWS-specific
  - Moving off managed service means rebuilding operational layer
  - Application code (Flink) stays portable
  - Lock-in is at operational layer, not business logic layer

Trade-off 3: Cost cross-over
  - For very large, steady, always-on workloads:
    self-managed on reserved instances may be cheaper
  - Model your specific usage pattern
  - Variable/spiky workloads favor managed auto-scaling
```

### How is managed Flink different from self-managed Flink?

| Feature | Managed Flink (AWS) | Self-Managed Flink |
|---|---|---|
| Infrastructure | AWS-managed, no servers | You provision and manage |
| Scaling | Auto-scaling KPUs | Manual cluster resize |
| Monitoring | CloudWatch built-in | Wire up Prometheus/Grafana |
| Checkpoints | S3 automatic | Configure storage yourself |
| High availability | Automatic | ZooKeeper/K8s setup |
| Flink version | AWS-curated (may lag) | Any version immediately |
| Customization | Curated options | Full control |
| Cost model | Pay-per-KPU-hour | Always-on cluster cost |
| Code portability | Same Flink API | Same Flink API |
| Operational lock-in | AWS-specific ops | Portable ops |

```java
// Code is identical on both:
env.addSource(kafkaOrKinesisSource)
    .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
    .process(new FraudRuleEngine())
    .addSink(alertSink);
// Only the infrastructure wrapper changes
```

### When should you choose managed Flink?

Choose managed Flink when your team is small and cannot staff cluster operations, when you need tight AWS integration, when your workload is variable or spiky, and when you do not need bleeding-edge Flink versions or deep customization.

```text
Decision guide:

  Small team, no platform engineers?    --> Managed Flink
  Tight AWS integration needed?         --> Managed Flink
  Variable/spiky workload?              --> Managed Flink (auto-scale)
  Need bleeding-edge Flink version?     --> Self-managed
  Need custom native plugins?           --> Self-managed
  Large, steady, always-on workload?    --> Model cost: may be self-managed
  Have a capable platform team?         --> Self-managed is an option
  Multi-cloud or cloud-agnostic goal?   --> Self-managed on K8s
```

### How do you mitigate vendor lock-in?

Keep the application code as clean Flink (DataStream, Table API, SQL) and isolate AWS-specific connectors and configuration. This way the business logic is portable and only the operational layer is AWS-specific.

```java
package com.bank.streaming;

// Portable Flink application code
public class PortableFraudPipeline {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
            StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

        // Source and sink are configured externally
        // On AWS: Kinesis source/sink
        // On self-managed: Kafka source/sink
        // Business logic is IDENTICAL:
        env.addSource(createSource(args))
            .keyBy(Transaction::getAccountId)
            .process(new FraudRuleEngine()) // portable logic
            .addSink(createSink(args));

        env.execute("NBI Fraud Pipeline");
    }

    // Factory methods isolate AWS-specific connectors
    private static SourceFunction<Transaction> createSource(
            String[] args) {
        if (isAws(args)) return new KinesisSource<>("nbi-txn");
        return new KafkaSource<>("nbi-txn");
    }

    private static SinkFunction<Alert> createSink(String[] args) {
        if (isAws(args)) return new KinesisSink<>("nbi-alerts");
        return new KafkaSink<>("nbi-alerts");
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with managed vs self-managed?

The most common mistake is defaulting to self-managed Flink without a capable platform team, spending more time on infrastructure than on business logic. Another mistake is ignoring the cost model and running a steady workload on pay-per-use when reserved instances would be cheaper.

```text
# WRONG: self-managed without platform team
# Result: team spends 60% of time on cluster ops
# Business logic delayed for months

# WRONG: managed Flink for huge steady workload
# 100 KPUs running 24/7 at fixed load
# Result: pay-per-use cost exceeds reserved instances

# CORRECT: managed for spiky workload, small team
# Auto-scales from 4 KPUs (normal) to 20 KPUs (peak)
# Team focuses on fraud logic, not cluster management

# CORRECT: self-managed for large steady workload + platform team
# Reserved instances, full control, lower cost at scale
```

### What should a senior engineer know about managed Flink?

A senior engineer should know that the right default for most teams is managed Flink, that they still own application-level tuning (watermarks, state TTL, parallelism) regardless of who runs the cluster, and that keeping the application as clean Flink code limits lock-in to the operational layer.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class SeniorManagedFlinkChecklist {
    // Senior engineer's managed Flink decision checklist:
    //
    // 1. Default: managed Flink (unless proven otherwise)
    // 2. You still own: watermarks, state TTL, parallelism,
    //    checkpoint interval, exactly-once semantics
    // 3. AWS handles: cluster provisioning, HA, patching,
    //    auto-scaling, monitoring infrastructure
    // 4. Mitigate lock-in: keep business logic as clean Flink,
    //    isolate AWS connectors in factory methods
    // 5. Model cost: compare KPU-hours vs reserved instances
    //    for your specific load profile
    // 6. Choose self-managed only when:
    //    - Need specific Flink version or custom plugin
    //    - Large steady workload where self-managed is cheaper
    //    - Have a capable platform team
    //    - Multi-cloud requirement
}
```
